      "text": "We start by understanding the absolute value of a complex number. The absolute value of a complex number \\( a + bi \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n|a + bi| = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, we have the complex number \\( -4 + ti \\). We need to find the value of \\( t \\) such that:\n\n\\[\n| -4 + ti | = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing the formula for the absolute value of a complex number:\n\n\\[\n| -4 + ti | = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2}\n\\]\n\nWe set this equal to \\(2\\sqrt{13}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(-4)^2 + t^2} = 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\]\n\nNext, we square both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[\n(-4)^2 + t^2 = (2\\sqrt{13})^2\n\\]\n\nWe calculate each term separately:\n\n\\[\n(-4)^2 = 16\n\\]\n\\[\n(2\\sqrt{13})^2 = 4 \\cdot 13 = 52\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n16 + t^2 = 52\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( t^2 \\), we subtract 16 from both sides:\n\n\\[\nt^2 = 52 - 16\n\\]\n\\[\nt^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = \\pm 6\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\( t \\), we have:\n\n\\[\nt = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive value of \\( t \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
